("h2", "3.1 Mass in Physical and Chemical Changes"),
("ul", ["Hydrogen (combustible) and oxygen (supports burning) combine to form water, which does neither — the properties of a compound differ from its elements — yet the **mass of water formed equals the sum of the masses of hydrogen and oxygen**.",
 "**Activity 3.1 (physical change):** weigh water + salt on a digital balance (tare set to zero first), then swirl until dissolved. The mass is unchanged — no change in mass in a physical change (also tearing paper). Variation of ±1 in the last digit is experimental error."]),
("h3", "Activity 3.2 — Baking soda and vinegar"),
("table", ["", "Set-up 1 (open)", "Set-up 2 (closed with balloon)"], [
 ["Arrangement", "Vinegar in flask; baking soda in balloon placed beside it; baking soda then poured in", "Balloon with baking soda fixed on mouth of flask holding vinegar; soda tipped in"],
 ["Observation", "Brisk effervescence; final reading **less** than initial", "Balloon inflates; final reading **equals** initial"],
 ["Reason", "CO<sub>2</sub> gas escapes into the air", "CO<sub>2</sub> is trapped in the balloon, nothing escapes"]]),
("note", "REMEMBER", "Vinegar + baking soda (sodium hydrogencarbonate) → carbon dioxide + other substances. Mass appears to drop only when a gaseous product escapes. Tip: keep the flask and balloon on the balance throughout so stray traces do not cause errors."),

("h2", "3.2 Law of Conservation of Mass"),
("note", "DEFINITION", "**Law of Conservation of Mass (Lavoisier, 1789):** Matter can neither be created nor destroyed in a chemical reaction — the total mass of reactants equals the total mass of products. Lavoisier is called the **Father of Modern Chemistry**."),
("ul", ["**Activity 3.3:** 10 mL of 1 % m/v sodium sulfate (flask A) and 10 mL of 1 % m/v barium chloride (flask B) are weighed together, then mixed. A white precipitate of **barium sulfate** forms along with sodium chloride: sodium sulfate + barium chloride → barium sulfate + sodium chloride. The total mass is unchanged. Keep both flasks on the balance to avoid loss from solution sticking to walls.",
 "Zinc + dilute HCl → zinc chloride + hydrogen: in an open container hydrogen escapes; to test the law you must use a closed system.",
 "Burning 10 g ethanol in an open beaker leaves no residue, but the law is **not violated**: the products (CO<sub>2</sub> and water vapour) escape into the air, and total mass of reactants (ethanol + oxygen) equals total mass of products."]),
("h3", "Numericals"),
("table", ["Problem", "Working", "Result"], [
 ["4.0 g CaCO<sub>3</sub> + 2.92 g HCl → 1.76 g CO<sub>2</sub> + 0.72 g H<sub>2</sub>O + 4.44 g CaCl<sub>2</sub>", "Reactants 4.0 + 2.92 = 6.92 g; products 1.76 + 0.72 + 4.44 = 6.92 g", "Law obeyed"],
 ["12 g C + 32 g O<sub>2</sub> → 44 g CO<sub>2</sub>; how much CO<sub>2</sub> from 2.4 g C?", "44/12 × 2.4", "8.8 g"],
 ["20 g H<sub>2</sub> + 160 g O<sub>2</sub> → water", "20 + 160", "180 g water"],
 ["5.3 g Na<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> + 6.0 g acetic acid → 2.2 g CO<sub>2</sub> + 0.9 g H<sub>2</sub>O + 8.2 g sodium acetate", "Left 11.3 g; right 2.2 + 0.9 + 8.2 = 11.3 g", "Law valid"]]),

("h2", "3.3 Law of Constant (Definite) Proportions"),
("note", "DEFINITION", "**Law of Constant Proportions (Proust’s law):** In any chemical compound, the elements are always present in a **fixed ratio by mass**, irrespective of source or method of preparation."),
("ul", ["Water from rivers, borewells or the ocean, when purified and decomposed, always gives hydrogen and oxygen in the mass ratio **1 : 8** (9 g water → 1 g H + 8 g O).",
 "Proust showed copper carbonate always has Cu : C : O in fixed proportion. Cinnabar (*hingula*), the red ore known in many ancient civilisations, yields mercury (≈ 86.22 %) and sulfur (≈ 13.78 %) on heating; grinding them in this ratio re-forms it (though toxicity limited this)."]),
("table", ["Problem", "Working", "Answer"], [
 ["NaCl has Na : Cl = 23 : 35.5. Chlorine needed for 46 g Na?", "35.5 ÷ 23 × 46", "71 g"],
 ["Compound 40 % S, 60 % O. Oxygen with 20 g S?", "S : O = 2 : 3, so 20 × 3/2", "30 g"],
 ["CO has C : O = 3 : 4. Oxygen for 9 g C?", "9 × 4/3", "12 g"],
 ["Copper oxide made as 4 : 1 (X) and 8 : 2 (Y)", "8 : 2 simplifies to 4 : 1", "Same ratio — law is supported"]]),
("note", "IMPORTANT", "The law holds for **compounds** but not for **mixtures**, whose components can be mixed in any proportion (e.g. sugar and water in a solution)."),

("h2", "3.4 Dalton’s Atomic Theory"),
("ul", ["The two laws were explained by **John Dalton** (taught in Manchester; presented his theory in **1808**). A **postulate** is a fundamental assumption accepted without proof from which ideas are developed."]),
("ol", ["All matter is made up of very tiny particles called **atoms**, which take part in chemical reactions.",
 "Atoms are **indivisible** and cannot be created or destroyed in a chemical reaction.",
 "Atoms of a given element are **identical** in mass and chemical properties.",
 "Atoms of different elements have **different** masses and chemical properties.",
 "Atoms combine in the ratio of **simple whole numbers** to form compounds.",
 "The relative number and kinds of atoms are **constant** in a given compound."]),
("table", ["Postulate", "Explains"], [["Atoms are neither created nor destroyed, only rearranged", "Law of Conservation of Mass"], ["Fixed number and kind of atoms in a compound", "Law of Constant Proportions"]]),
("ul", ["Examples: magnesium burning in air gives white magnesium oxide (Mg atoms combine with O atoms). H and O atoms combine to form water without being destroyed.",
 "Later work showed atoms are divisible (electrons, protons, neutrons) — see Chapter 4. Assertion–reason: 2 g H + 16 g O → 18 g water is true (ratio 1 : 8) and Dalton’s whole-number combining of atoms explains it, so both true and R is the correct explanation."]),

("h2", "3.5 How Atoms Combine"),
("ul", ["A **molecule** is an electrically neutral entity of more than one atom that can exist independently and shows all the properties of that substance. Molecules of elements (H<sub>2</sub>, O<sub>2</sub>) have atoms of one kind; molecules of compounds (HCl, H<sub>2</sub>O) have different kinds. Noble gases such as helium exist as single atoms because they are already stable.",
 "Stability = full valence shell: **octet** (8 electrons) or **duplet** (2 when K is the outer shell). Atoms with fewer electrons **share, gain or lose** electrons — in two ways: **sharing** (covalent) and **transfer** (ionic).",
 "Combination lowers the energy of the system below that of separate atoms, which makes it stable. The force holding atoms together is a **chemical bond**."]),
("h3", "Covalent bond — bonding by sharing"),
("table", ["Molecule", "Valence electrons needed", "Sharing", "Bond", "Structure"], [
 ["Hydrogen H<sub>2</sub>", "1 each (to complete duplet)", "1 pair", "Single", "H—H"],
 ["Chlorine Cl<sub>2</sub>", "1 each (has 7)", "1 pair", "Single", "Cl—Cl"],
 ["Oxygen O<sub>2</sub>", "2 each (has 6)", "2 pairs", "Double", "O=O"],
 ["Nitrogen N<sub>2</sub>", "3 each (has 5)", "3 pairs", "Triple", "N≡N"],
 ["Hydrogen chloride HCl", "H needs 1, Cl needs 1", "1 pair", "Single", "H—Cl"],
 ["Water H<sub>2</sub>O", "O needs 2, each H needs 1", "O shares with two H atoms", "2 single", "H—O—H"]]),
("ul", ["A **covalent bond** is formed by a shared pair of electrons that attracts both nuclei. Other examples: CO<sub>2</sub> (O=C=O), NH<sub>3</sub> (N bonded to three H), H<sub>2</sub>S, F<sub>2</sub> (fluorine, atomic number 9, 2,7: one shared pair).",
 "Neon (2, 8) neither shares nor transfers electrons because its octet is already complete."]),
("h3", "Naming covalent compounds"),
("ul", ["Use prefixes for the number of atoms: mono-1, di-2, tri-3, tetra-4, penta-5, hexa-6. The first element keeps its name (no *mono-* on it); the second ends in **-ide**.",
 "Drop the vowel of the prefix before an element starting with a vowel (**monoxide**, **pentoxide**) but keep ‘i’ (**dioxide**, **trioxide**). Hydrogen as the first element never takes a prefix (H<sub>2</sub>S = hydrogen sulfide).",
 "Some compounds keep common names: H<sub>2</sub>O (water), NH<sub>3</sub> (ammonia)."]),
("table", ["Formula", "Name", "Formula", "Name"], [["CO", "Carbon monoxide", "SF<sub>6</sub>", "Sulfur hexafluoride"], ["CO<sub>2</sub>", "Carbon dioxide", "N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub>", "Dinitrogen tetroxide"], ["CS<sub>2</sub>", "Carbon disulfide", "N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>5</sub>", "Dinitrogen pentoxide"], ["PCl<sub>3</sub>", "Phosphorus trichloride", "NO<sub>2</sub>", "Nitrogen dioxide"]]),
("h3", "Ionic bond — bonding by transfer"),
("ul", ["Atoms with **fewer than 4** valence electrons usually **lose** them; those with **more than 4** usually **gain or share**.",
 "**NaCl:** sodium (2, 8, 1) loses 1 electron → **Na<sup>+</sup> cation** (11 protons, 10 electrons). Chlorine (2, 8, 7) gains 1 → **Cl<sup>−</sup> anion**. Oppositely charged ions attract by an electrostatic force — the **ionic bond**.",
 "Sulfur gains 2 electrons → S<sup>2−</sup>; oxygen → O<sup>2−</sup>; magnesium loses 2 → Mg<sup>2+</sup>. Mg<sup>2+</sup> + 2 Cl<sup>−</sup> → MgCl<sub>2</sub>; 2 Na<sup>+</sup> + S<sup>2−</sup> → Na<sub>2</sub>S; Ca → Ca<sup>2+</sup>, K → K<sup>+</sup>.",
 "Ionic compounds form **3-D crystals** (crystal lattice): in NaCl each Na<sup>+</sup> is surrounded by six Cl<sup>−</sup> and each Cl<sup>−</sup> by six Na<sup>+</sup>. They do not form separate molecules.",
 "Ionic compounds generally form between **metals and non-metals**. Name the cation first, then the anion (simple anions end in -ide, polyatomic ions usually do not)."]),
("table", ["Covalent bond", "Ionic bond"], [["Sharing of electrons", "Transfer of electrons"], ["Between non-metal atoms", "Between a metal and a non-metal"], ["Neutral molecules (H<sub>2</sub>O, HCl)", "Ions in a 3-D crystal (NaCl)"]]),
("note", "IMPORTANT", "Nuclear (atomic) energy arises when nuclei split or combine — used in power generation, medicine, research and space. **Raja Ramanna** is called the Father of the Indian Nuclear Programme (peaceful use)."),

